**Short version:** a switch connects devices *within* one network; a router connects *different* networks and picks the path between them.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("Router vs. switch", [rt, sw])
rt = {name:"Router", attributes:["Layer 3 — network layer","Forwards by IP address","Joins separate networks (LAN ↔ internet)","Does NAT, DHCP, firewall, VPN","Few ports (1 WAN + 2-4 LAN)","Slower per port — must decide a route"]}
sw = {name:"Switch", attributes:["Layer 2 — data link (L3 switches exist)","Forwards by MAC address","Connects devices inside one LAN","Just forwards frames — no NAT/routing","Many ports (8-48+), plus uplinks","Very fast, ASIC/hardware forwarding"]}
:::

They're complementary rather than competing: inside your house, the switch fans the connection out to your devices; the router is the single door out to the internet and the thing that assigns addresses and blocks unsolicited traffic. Home "wireless routers" are just a router, a switch, and an access point in one box — which is why the distinction gets blurry in practice.

Pick by question asked: *"how do I get more Ethernet ports?"* → switch. *"how do I connect two networks or reach the internet?"* → router.

